<h1>Web accessibility certificate</h1>
<p class="lede">One of the questions we get most. The short answer: certificates do exist, none of them is mandatory, and none of them is exactly what the law asks you for.</p>

<div class="prose">
  <h2>What the law asks for is not called a certificate</h2>
  <p>Neither European nor US accessibility law sets up a body that certifies websites, or an official seal you have to display. What they require is that your product conforms, and that you can show it. In Europe the document with a name of its own is the <strong>accessibility statement</strong>. In the United States, what procurement asks for is an <strong>ACR</strong>. Neither is a certificate.</p>
  <p>If you want to know which framework covers you and since when, we explain it in <a href="/en/regulations/">which accessibility law applies to you</a>.</p>

  <h2>So are the certificates on sale worthless?</h2>
  <p>No, and it is worth being precise about this, because there is a lot of noise around it. Serious voluntary certification schemes do exist. In Spain, AENOR certifies web accessibility against UNE-EN 301549 and WCAG at levels AA and AAA, with a review every six months to check the site is still accessible over time.</p>
  <p>What matters is understanding what those schemes are: <strong>voluntary third-party verification</strong>. They are useful as proof to a client or in a public tender, and they do not replace the legal obligation, because the obligation is to conform and to document it.</p>
  <p>And there is one difference that does matter: <strong>what holds any seal up is the evaluation behind it</strong>. A badge you get by installing a script on your site, appearing minutes later, has evaluated nothing. There is no automated way to check whether an image's alternative text describes what the image shows, whether the tab order makes sense, or whether an error message is announced when it appears. That gets checked by hand, with a keyboard and a screen reader, or it does not get checked.</p>

  <h2>What an evaluation that holds up looks like</h2>
  <p>The W3C publishes a methodology for this, WCAG-EM. It defines how to choose which pages get evaluated, how to record each criterion and how to express the result, so that someone else can repeat the evaluation and reach the same conclusion. It is what a serious report is expected to follow.</p>
  <p>WCAG-EM works on a structured sample of the site. In a tuaccesible site audit we go further: we review page by page and link by link, not a sample. The reason is simple: a sample is enough to estimate the state of a site; the whole site is what backs a statement with no gaps in it.</p>

  <h2>Which document you need, depending on where you sell</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>United States.</strong> An <strong>ACR</strong>, short for Accessibility Conformance Report. It is the <strong>VPAT</strong> template filled in for your product: criterion by criterion, whether it supports, partially supports, does not support or does not apply, with remarks. The vendor reports it under their own responsibility, and it is what public procurement and large companies ask for before buying software.</li>
    <li><strong>Europe.</strong> An accessibility statement backed by an evaluation against WCAG and EN 301 549. It is what anyone reviewing your compliance will ask to see.</li>
    <li><strong>Latin America.</strong> It depends on the country, and in most of them the obligation reaches the public sector. If you sell into Europe or the United States, their rules apply to you wherever your company is based.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What we deliver</h2>
  <p>We do not issue seals. We deliver the evaluation any of those documents rests on, and the documents themselves:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>An audit against WCAG 2.2, Level AA as the minimum, with the criteria table one by one.</li>
    <li>The accessibility statement drafted and ready to publish.</li>
    <li>The ACR, on its own or alongside the full report with screenshots and examples.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>If you later want to go through a third-party certification scheme, the work is already done: what those schemes review is exactly this.</p>
